imental models of transmissible spongiform encephalopathy</article-title><source>Transfusion</source><year>1998</year><volume>38</volume><fpage>810</fpage><lpage>816</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9738619</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1046/j.1537-2995.1998.38998408999.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Cervenakova</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>McShane</surname><given-names>LM</given-names></name><name><surname>Barber</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Rubenstein</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Drohan</surname><given-names>WN</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Further studies of blood infectivity in an experimental model of transmissible spongiform encephalopathy, with an explanation of why blood components do not transmit Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease in humans</article-title><source>Transfusion</source><year>1999</year><volume>39</volume><fpage>1169</fpage><lpage>1178</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10604242</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1046/j.1537-2995.1999.39111169.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Foster</surname><given-names>PR</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Prions and blood products</article-title><source>Ann Med</source><year>2000</year><volume>32</volume><fpage>501</fpage><lpage>513</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11087171</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Foster</surname><given-names>PR</given-names></name><name><surname>Welch</surname><given-names>AG</given-names></name><name><surname>McLean</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Griffin</surname><given-names>B